<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android Auto Backup rules for the container app — what Google's backup transport carries off the
  phone on its own. This is a different thing from the Operations Sandbox's own zip
  (docs/OPERATIONS_SANDBOX.md): that archive is one somebody *takes*, and this one happens to them.

  ## Why these name domains rather than files

  They used to name files. When the container held two apps they read `lifeops.db` and
  `citation.db`, and that was the whole truth. Nine databases arrived afterwards — health, people,
  project, maintenance, finance, logistics, repository, advisor and advisor's memory — and not one
  of them was ever added here, because nothing failed when it wasn't. A cloud restore therefore
  brought back two apps' data, *everybody's* preferences, and nine empty apps sitting behind
  settings that described data that was gone.

  So the database domain is taken whole. A rule that names the domain cannot fall behind the apps
  the way a rule that names files did, and `AutoBackupRulesTest` is what proves it hasn't: it makes
  every app create its real database and fails if the rules here would leave one on the phone.

  `filesDir` is deliberately in neither list. Citation's books, Advisor's models and the household's
  documents live there, they are measured in hundreds of megabytes, and Auto Backup's quota is 25 —
  an app over it is not trimmed, it is skipped. Those files are the sandbox zip's job.

  ## Why anything is excluded

  Every credential store in the suite, and for two reasons that both end the same way.

  They are `EncryptedSharedPreferences`: a Tink keyset wrapped by a hardware-bound Android Keystore
  key that is never part of a backup and is regenerated on reinstall. Restore the file and the
  surviving keyset meets a key that cannot open it — `AEADBadTagException`, straight out of
  `EncryptedSharedPreferences.create`. Citation and the container rebuild the store empty rather
  than dying on launch, but an unreadable file is still worth nothing, and it is worth nothing on
  purpose. The two `_plain` fallbacks (written only on a device whose keystore is unusable) fail the
  other way: they hold the credential in the clear, which is not a thing to hand to somebody else's
  backup transport.

  Neither is a loss. The suite's own vault carries these onto the new phone — see docs/SECRETS.md —
  which is the whole reason it exists.

  A WAL checkpoint runs when the app is backgrounded (see LifeOpsApp) so lifeops.db is current;
  Citation's db is checkpointed at backup time by its contributor.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="database" path="." />
    <include domain="sharedpref" path="." />

    <!-- Citation: the O'Reilly library card and PIN, and the OPDS catalogue sign-ins. -->
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="oreilly_access.xml" />
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="opds_catalog_access.xml" />
    <!-- Finance: the bank and aggregator tokens. -->
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="secure_finance_access.xml" />
    <!-- Secrets: the vault key wrapped by *this* phone's Keystore for the fingerprint shortcut. A
         backup holding both it and the vault would be a backup holding the vault in plaintext. -->
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="secure_secrets_device.xml" />
    <!-- The container's own: the updater's GitHub token, and the signature the scheduled archive
         uploads with. Both are mirrored into the vault and come back from there. -->
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="sandbox_update_secrets.xml" />
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="sandbox_update_secrets_plain.xml" />
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="sandbox_cloud_backup_secrets.xml" />
    <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="sandbox_cloud_backup_secrets_plain.xml" />
</full-backup-content>
